---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageIntro from "../components/PageIntro.astro";
import SectionBlock from "../components/SectionBlock.astro";
import CardGrid from "../components/CardGrid.astro";
import CardItem from "../components/CardItem.astro";

const pageTitle = "Low-leverage work — Ethotechnics";
const pageDescription =
  "Familiar projects that improve the quality of an exercise of power while leaving the structure of that power untouched, and the questions that replace them.";
const anchorLinks = [
  { href: "#criterion", label: "The criterion" },
  { href: "#catalog", label: "The catalog" },
  { href: "#permanent-question", label: "The permanent question" },
];

const items = [
  {
    title: "Perfecting representation",
    pattern:
      "Effort flows into accuracy, coverage, and personalization as though enough fidelity will dissolve the political problem. It will not: even perfect prediction would not itself establish jurisdiction.",
    question:
      "Preserve standing for when representation fails — or when it succeeds but should not be authoritative.",
    href: "/research/theory/model-of-a-person",
    linkLabel: "The model of a person is not the person",
  },
  {
    title: "Metric wars without institutional redesign",
    pattern:
      "Years can go into making the ledger more precise while the ledger's boundary stays untouched. Accurate accounting within a false boundary still produces a false account.",
    question:
      "Whose costs does the metric exclude, and who carries what it cannot see?",
    href: "/glossary/falsified-denominator",
    linkLabel: "Falsified denominator",
  },
  {
    title: "Transparency as a substitute for power",
    pattern:
      "Dashboards, model cards, and disclosure notices explain the system while leaving it unchallengeable. Knowing why you were harmed is not being able to reverse it. Legibility is not standing.",
    question:
      "What state can the explanation change, and who can trigger that change?",
    href: "/standards/laws#law-viii",
    linkLabel: "Law VIII: observability without state transition is theater",
  },
  {
    title: "Human-in-the-loop theater",
    pattern:
      "A human clicking approve at the end of a machine-generated workflow, without time, information, authority, protection, or reversal, does not constitutionalize the system.",
    question:
      "Can anyone actually interrupt, override, investigate, and change the rule?",
    href: "/glossary/meaningful-control",
    linkLabel: "Meaningful control",
  },
  {
    title: "Training better decision-makers instead of changing dependencies",
    pattern:
      "Workshops, codes, and exhortations improve conduct while people remain dependent on somebody's goodwill. Better character is not a substitute for better structure.",
    question:
      "Which dependency can be redesigned so virtue is no longer the load-bearing part?",
    href: "/research/theory/dependence-without-standing",
    linkLabel: "Dependence without standing",
  },
  {
    title: "Participation without consequence",
    pattern:
      "Citizen panels, employee feedback, and stakeholder workshops consume enormous energy while decision rules stay intact. If participants can force nothing, participation has become a sink for dissent.",
    question:
      "What can participants actually force the institution to reconsider?",
    href: "/glossary/corrective-standing",
    linkLabel: "Corrective standing",
  },
  {
    title: "Optimizing efficiency before fixing the denominator",
    pattern:
      "A workflow that gets faster by pushing friction onto patients, claimants, contractors, or support staff has optimized burden transfer. Do not optimize a system until you know where its residuals go.",
    question:
      "Where do the residuals land, and does the ledger count them?",
    href: "/research/theory/absorption-as-concealment",
    linkLabel: "Absorption as concealment",
  },
  {
    title: "Debating abstract values while jurisdiction expands",
    pattern:
      "Years negotiating principles — fairness, beneficence, responsibility — while deployments move into progressively more intimate domains. A technically excellent system performing an unjustified function is badly designed.",
    question:
      "Why is this institution entitled to make this inference or decision at all?",
    href: "/research/theory/what-does-not-convert",
    linkLabel: "What does not convert",
  },
  {
    title: "Treating every failure as a local bug",
    pattern:
      "Repeated residuals get case-by-case repairs, better support, more patches. At some threshold, many exceptions are evidence against the rule or representation that produces them.",
    question:
      "Has the exception pattern been read as evidence about the architecture?",
    href: "/evals/corrective-learning",
    linkLabel: "Corrective Learning evals",
  },
  {
    title: "The autonomy debate",
    pattern:
      "Whether the machine is agentic, aligned, or controllable matters less than the institutional relationship built around it. A perfectly obedient algorithm can carry extraordinary unanswerable power.",
    question: "Machine obedience is not human non-domination. What holds the operator answerable?",
    href: "/research/theory/democratic-vs-coercive-governability",
    linkLabel: "Democratic vs. coercive governability",
  },
  {
    title: "Eliminating uncertainty instead of governing it",
    pattern:
      "One more dataset, one better model, one more sensor — imagined as finally removing the need for judgment, contestation, and exception handling. The mature goal is not complete representation.",
    question:
      "Does the system remain legitimate when it inevitably does not know?",
    href: "/glossary/safe-incompleteness",
    linkLabel: "Safe incompleteness",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Leverage"
    title="Low-leverage work"
    description={pageDescription}
    anchorLinks={anchorLinks}
  />

  <SectionBlock
    id="criterion"
    eyebrow="The criterion"
    title="Better at deciding is not the same as entitled to decide"
    description="A project is low-leverage when it makes the institution better at exercising power instead of asking whether, how, and under what conditions it should exercise that power at all."
  >
    <p>
      None of the projects in the catalog below is worthless in itself. Metric
      precision can matter. Explanation can matter. Participation can matter.
      Each becomes a sink when it substitutes for the structural question —
      when enormous effort improves the quality of an exercise of power while
      leaving the structure of that power untouched.
    </p>
    <p>
      The recurring mistake takes one form:
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      Make the institution better at deciding, instead of asking whether, how,
      and under what conditions it should decide.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="catalog"
    eyebrow="The catalog"
    title="Eleven familiar projects, and what replaces them"
    description="Each card names the pattern and the question that restores leverage. The links open the part of the framework built for that question."
  >
    <CardGrid className="grid--two">
      {
        items.map((item) => (
          <CardItem title={item.title} description={item.pattern}>
            <p class="muted small">
              <strong>Replace with:</strong> {item.question}
            </p>
            <a slot="footer" class="muted" href={item.href}>
              {item.linkLabel} →
            </a>
          </CardItem>
        ))
      }
    </CardGrid>
    <p class="small muted">
      Two absences are deliberate. Nothing here says expertise is worthless —
      expertise stays authoritative within the jurisdiction it is qualified to
      answer, and loses only the conversion from knowing more to deciding. And
      nothing here says efficiency is worthless — the objection is to
      efficiency claims whose denominator excludes the people doing the
      absorbing. See also{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/abstention">abstention</a>, the capability of
      declining to decide, which most of these projects substitute for.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>

  <SectionBlock
    id="permanent-question"
    eyebrow="The permanent question"
    title="Whatever arrangement you favor, what happens when it gets someone wrong?"
    description="The test beneath the catalog. No arrangement escapes it by procedure, ownership, expertise, participation, or intent."
    variant="alt"
  >
    <p>
      Markets do not escape it. States do not escape it. Democratic majorities,
      experts, workers' councils, and models do not escape it. No arrangement
      possesses constitutive innocence: nothing becomes non-dominating merely
      because the right people own it, because it is public, because it was
      designed by experts, or because its goals are emancipatory. Those facts
      matter. They do not settle the question.
    </p>
    <p>
      The requirement is permanent: power must remain corrigible by the reality
      on which it acts. The exception must be able to travel back into the rule
      that produced it, the challenge must be affordable to the person who
      carries it, and the decision must stay reopenable — which is what{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/non-finality">non-finality</a> names. Everything on
      this site is machinery for one version of that requirement, and the
      deeper version of the whole project is the same sentence pointed forward:
      how to build systems that can become more capable without becoming more
      entitled to rule.
    </p>
    <p class="small muted">
      The underlying epistemology is stated in{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/excluded-case">the excluded case</a> and{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/consequential-contradiction"
        >consequential contradiction</a
      >; the design stance is{" "}
      <a href="/glossary/safe-incompleteness">safe incompleteness</a>.
    </p>
  </SectionBlock>
</BaseLayout>
